<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    CraftLauncher entitlements.

    Kept to the minimum that is valid for every signing path, because an
    entitlement the signer cannot issue does not degrade gracefully — it makes the
    whole signature fail, or gets stripped silently and leaves the app running with
    privileges it never had.

    ── What each key does here ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

    get-task-allow
        Lets a debugger attach to the process. SideStore / AltStore / SideJITServer
        rely on exactly this to hand out JIT, and Xcode needs it for a Debug build.
        Free and paid developer certificates both accept it.
        Note: a distribution or App Store profile will reject it, which is expected
        — this app is not distributable through the App Store anyway.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        Required to `dlopen` a library that was not signed by the same team as the
        app. The Java runtime ships its own dylibs, so without this the JVM cannot
        load and the launch dies at `dlopen(libjli.dylib)`.
        A jailbreak or TrollStore grants this implicitly; on a stock device with a
        personal certificate it is accepted, which is why it stays in the base set.

    ── Deliberately absent ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    com.apple.private.memorystatus
    com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing
        Privileged. They raise the jetsam memory ceiling and unlock a large virtual
        address space (which Java 17 wants for compressed class pointers). Only a
        jailbreak, TrollStore, or a special Apple grant can issue them.
        LauncherPipeline probes for them at runtime and adapts:
          * memory entitlement missing  → allocation capped at 25% of physical RAM
            instead of 40%
          * extended-VA missing         → adds -XX:-UseCompressedClassPointers
        So the app works without them. Uncomment the two keys below ONLY if you are
        signing on a jailbroken device with a tool that can inject them
        (全能签 / 轻松签 on a jailbroken device can; on a stock device they will
        break the signature).

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        Removed on purpose: these are macOS hardened-runtime keys. They have no
        meaning on iOS, where JIT is gated by the kernel's own
        `dynamic-codesigning` entitlement (plus the CS_DEBUGGED flag a debugger
        attach sets). Carrying them here only adds a way for signing to fail.

        <key>com.apple.private.memorystatus</key>
        <true/>
        <key>com.apple.developer.kernel.extended-virtual-addressing</key>
        <true/>
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
